{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs GNU C++11 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following GNU C++11 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nSometimes one has to spell email addresses over the phone. Then one usually pronounces a dot as dot, an at sign as at. As a result, we get something like vasyaatgmaildotcom. Your task is to transform it into a proper email address (vasya@gmail.com). It is known that a proper email address contains only such symbols as . @ and lower-case Latin letters, doesn't start with and doesn't end with a dot. Also, a proper email address doesn't start with and doesn't end with an at sign. Moreover, an email address contains exactly one such symbol as @, yet may contain any number (possible, zero) of dots. You have to carry out a series of replacements so that the length of the result was as short as possible and it was a proper email address. If the lengths are equal, you should print the lexicographically minimal result. Overall, two variants of replacement are possible: dot can be replaced by a dot, at can be replaced by an at. \n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the GNU C++11 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\n#include <cstring>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    string s;\n    cin >> s;\n    \n    string out;\n    if (s.size() > 0) out += s[0];\n    \n    bool at_done = false;\n    int i = 1;\n    \n    while (i < s.length()) {\n        if (i + 2 < s.length() && \n            s[i] == 'd' && s[i+1] == 'o' && s[i+2] == 't') {\n            out += '.';\n            i += 3;\n        }\n        else if (i + 1 < s.length() && \n                 !at_done && \n                 s[i] == 'a' && s[i+1] == 't') {\n            out += '@';\n            at_done = true;\n            i += 2;\n        }\n        else {\n            i++;\n        }\n    }\n    \n    cout << out << endl;\n    return 0;\n}\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    string s;\n    cin >> s;\n    \n    // We need to find exactly one '@' and any number of '.' \n    // The idea: try all possible positions for '@' (where \"at\" appears)\n    // and for each, try all possible positions for '.' (where \"dot\" appears)\n    // such that the resulting string is valid\n    \n    vector<pair<int,int>> at_positions; // positions where \"at\" starts\n    vector<int> dot_positions; // positions where \"dot\" starts\n    \n    for (int i = 0; i < (int)s.length(); i++) {\n        if (i + 1 < (int)s.length() && s[i] == 'a' && s[i+1] == 't') {\n            at_positions.push_back(i);\n        }\n        if (i + 2 < (int)s.length() && s[i] == 'd' && s[i+1] == 'o' && s[i+2] == 't') {\n            dot_positions.push_back(i);\n        }\n    }\n    \n    string best_result = \"\";\n    \n    // Try each possible '@' position\n    for (int at_idx : at_positions) {\n        // Build the string with '@' at this position\n        string candidate = \"\";\n        int pos = 0;\n        \n        // Add characters before '@'\n        while (pos < at_idx) {\n            if (pos + 2 < (int)s.length() && s[pos] == 'd' && s[pos+1] == 'o' && s[pos+2] == 't') {\n                candidate += '.';\n                pos += 3;\n            } else {\n                candidate += s[pos];\n                pos++;\n            }\n        }\n        candidate += '@';\n        pos = at_idx + 2; // skip \"at\"\n        \n        // Add characters after '@'\n        while (pos < (int)s.length()) {\n            if (pos + 2 < (int)s.length() && s[pos] == 'd' && s[pos+1] == 'o' && s[pos+2] == 't') {\n                candidate += '.';\n                pos += 3;\n            } else {\n                candidate += s[pos];\n                pos++;\n            }\n        }\n        \n        // Check if valid email\n        bool valid = true;\n        \n        // Must have exactly one '@'\n        int at_count = 0;\n        for (char c : candidate) {\n            if (c == '@') at_count++;\n        }\n        if (at_count != 1) valid = false;\n        \n        // Must not start or end with '.' or '@'\n        if (candidate.empty() || candidate[0] == '.' || candidate[0] == '@' || \n            candidate.back() == '.' || candidate.back() == '@') {\n            valid = false;\n        }\n        \n        // Must contain only lowercase letters, '.', and '@'\n        for (char c : candidate) {\n            if (!((c >= 'a' && c <= 'z') || c == '.' || c == '@')) {\n                valid = false;\n                break;\n            }\n        }\n        \n        // Check no consecutive dots\n        for (int i = 0; i < (int)candidate.length() - 1; i++) {\n            if (candidate[i] == '.' && candidate[i+1] == '.') {\n                valid = false;\n                break;\n            }\n        }\n        \n        if (valid) {\n            if (best_result == \"\" || candidate.length() < best_result.length() || \n                (candidate.length() == best_result.length() && candidate < best_result)) {\n                best_result = candidate;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    \n    // If no valid result found with the above approach, try to handle edge cases\n    // Actually, the problem states that we need to replace \"dot\" with '.' and \"at\" with '@'\n    // So we should also consider that there might be multiple \"at\" occurrences but we need exactly one '@'\n    // The above loop handles that by trying each \"at\" as the single '@'\n    \n    // But what if there's no \"at\" in the string? Then we cannot form a valid email\n    // According to the problem, we should carry out replacements to get a valid email\n    // So there must be at least one \"at\" in the input\n    \n    cout << best_result << endl;\n    return 0;\n}\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.85\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-8.344646857949556e-07, -0.00033241944038309157, -2.50339189733495e-06, -5.960462772236497e-07, -4.172316494077677e-06, 0.0, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -6.103329360485077e-05, 0.0, -1.311301275563892e-06, -4.768370445162873e-07, -0.020546805113554, 0.0, -0.39414963126182556, -9.536738616588991e-07, -0.04300813376903534, -4.768370445162873e-07, -0.6457861065864563, -8.940656698541716e-06, -0.6326190829277039, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -2.145764938177308e-06, -7.152555099310121e-07, -0.014545004814863205, -4.005352093372494e-05, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -8.344646857949556e-07, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -3.576278118089249e-07, -9.536738616588991e-07, -2.002696055569686e-05, -4.768370445162873e-07, -2.455681169521995e-05, -3.576278118089249e-07, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -6.389413465512916e-05, -8.344646857949556e-07, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -0.6777462959289551, -0.6551628112792969, -0.001992385368794203, -1.3708974620385561e-05, -0.0866725966334343, -1.005852460861206, -0.0006418551784008741, -0.16416844725608826, -3.6954811548639555e-06, -0.3418694734573364, -0.09921814501285553, -6.079655122448457e-06, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -0.20697474479675